#: Game.Levels.Intermediate.L02_Two
msgid "## Summary\n"
"\n"
"If §0 is a hypothesis and the goal is §1, then\n"
"§2 changes the goal to §3.\n"
"\n"
"## Details\n"
"\n"
"If you have a function §4 and your goal is §5\n"
"then §6 changes the goal to §7. The logic is\n"
"simple: if you are trying to create a term of type §8,\n"
"but §9 is a function that turns terms of type §10 into\n"
"terms of type §11, then it will suffice to construct a\n"
"term of type §12. A mathematician might say: 'We need\n"
"to construct an element of §13, but we have a function §14\n"
"so it suffices to construct an element of §15'. Or alternatively\n"
"'we need to prove §16, but we have a proof §17 that §18\n"
"so it suffices to prove §19'."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Intro.L02_Two
msgid "Multiplying by one on the left."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h`
#. §1: `X = Y`
#. §2: `rewrite [h]`
#. §3: `X`
#. §4: `Y`
#. §5: `rw`
#. §6: `rewrite`
#. §7: `rw [← h]`
#. §8: `←`
#. §9: `\\l`
#. §10: `Y`
#. §11: `X`
#. §12: `rw [h] at h2`
#. §13: `X`
#. §14: `Y`
#. §15: `h2`
#. §16: `rw`
#. §17: `h : A = B`
#. §18: `A = B`
#. §19: `A`
#. §20: `rw [h]`
#. §21: `B`
#. §22: `h : A ↔ B`
#. §23: `h`
#. §24: `A = B`
#. §25: `A ↔ B`
#. §26: `h`
#. §27: `rw`
#. §28: `rw (P = Q)`
#. §29: `P = Q`
#. §30: `h : P = Q`
#. §31: `rw [h]`
#. §32: `h : A = B`
#. §33: `B`
#. §34: `A`
#. §35: `rw [← h]`
#. §36: `\\l`
#. §37: ```
#. Objects:
#. A B C : Set X
#. Assumptions:
#. h : A = B ∪ C
#. Goal:
#.   A ∪ B = B ∪ C
#. ```
#. §38: `rw [h]`
#. §39: ` ⊢ B ∪ C ∪ B = B ∪ C`
#. §40: `rw`
#. §41: ```
#. Objects:
#. A B C D : Set X
#. Assumptions:
#. h1 : A = B ∩ C
#. h2 : B ∪ A = D
#. Goal:
#.   D = B
#. ```
#. §42: `rw [h1] at h2`
#. §43: `h2`
#. §44: `h2 : B ∪ B ∩ C = D`
#: Game.Levels.Intro.L01_First
msgid "## Summary\n"
"\n"
"If §0 is a proof of §1, then §2 will change\n"
"all §3s in the goal to §4s.\n"
"\n"
"As this is such a common proof step we also have a short name, §5 instead of §6 for this\n"
"step, to save us from too much typing.\n"
"\n"
"Variants: §7 (type §8 using §9 for left) changes\n"
"§10 to §11 and\n"
"§12 changes §13 to §14 in hypothesis §15 instead\n"
"of the goal.\n"
"\n"
"## Details\n"
"\n"
"The §16 tactic is a way to do 'substituting in'.\n"
"\n"
"If §17 is in your local context (i.e., a proof of §18)\n"
"and if your goal contains one or more §19s, then §20\n"
"will change them all to §21's. This will also work if you have a proof of an if-and-only-if statement §22.\n"
"\n"
"Important note: if §23 is not a proof of the form §24\n"
"or §25 (for example if §26 is a function, an implication,\n"
"or perhaps even a proposition itself rather than its proof),\n"
"then §27 is not the tactic you want to use. For example,\n"
"§28 is never correct: §29 is the true-false\n"
"statement itself, not the proof.\n"
"If §30 is its proof, then §31 will work.\n"
"\n"
"Pro tip 1: If §32 and you want to change\n"
"§33s to §34s instead, try §35 (get the arrow with §36,\n"
"note that this is a small letter L, not a number 1).\n"
"\n"
"### Example:\n"
"If it looks like this in the bottom box:\n"
"§37\n"
"\n"
"then\n"
"\n"
"§38\n"
"\n"
"will change the goal into §39.\n"
"\n"
"### Example:\n"
"You can use §40 to change a hypothesis as well.\n"
"For example, if your local context looks like this:\n"
"§41\n"
"then §42 will turn §43 into §44 (remember operator precedence)."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Intermediate.L06_Six
msgid "The preimage of a set along the composition of maps."
msgstr ""

#: Game
msgid "You should use this game as a template for your own game and add your own levels."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rewrite`
#. §1: `rewrite`
#. §2: ```
#. Objects:
#. n m : ℕ
#. Assumptions:
#. h1 : n + 1 = 7
#. h2 : m = n + 1
#. Goal:
#. m + 2 = 9
#. ```
#. §3: `rewrite [h1] at h2`
#. §4: `h2`
#. §5: `h2 : m = 7`
#. §6: ``` lemma add_zero : ∀ x, x + 0 = x ```
#. §7: ``` lemma one_mul : ∀ x, 1 * x = x ```
#: Game.Levels.Intro.L02_Two
msgid "We can state lemmas assuming hypotheses with similar notation as we made a lemma\n"
"dependent on natural numbers before.\n"
"\n"
"The §0 tactic can then be used to rewrite a hypothesis. After all, we can substitute\n"
"things we know to be equal in facts we know, as well as substituting into what we are trying to prove.\n"
"\n"
"### Example:\n"
"You can use §1 to change a hypothesis as well.\n"
"For example, if your goal state looks like this:\n"
"§2\n"
"then §3 will turn §4 into §5.\n"
"\n"
"Below are two useful results you can use to finish this level.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"§6\n"
"\n"
"§7"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Intermediate.L05_Five
msgid "In this level you will prove the more complicated\n"
"identity (5) in <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commutator#Identities_(group_theory)>, called\n"
"the Hall-Witt identity."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `apply`
#. §1: $x ^ 3 + 7$
#. §2: $x$
#. §3: `fun`
#. §4: `fun`
#. §5: `fun x => x^3 + 7`
#. §6: `x`
#. §7: `x^3 + 7`
#. §8: `Continuous.add`
#. §9: `continuous_const`
#: Game.Levels.Intermediate.L02_Two
msgid "## Using apply\n"
"In this problem, you will show that a specific polynomial is continuous. You can do this using\n"
"the basic facts in the right sidebar: that adding continuous functions is continuous,\n"
"likewise multiplying continuous functions remains continuous, constant functions are continuous,\n"
"and the identity function is continuous. The way these lemmas are stated is very general, they work\n"
"for any continuous functions on arbitrary topological spaces, but by using §0 we can let Lean\n"
"work out the details automatically.\n"
"\n"
"But, how do we talk about the functions themselves?\n"
"The basic method to speak about an unnamed function in Lean makes use of lambda expressions.\n"
"In mathematics we might just write §1 to describe a polynomial function, leaving it\n"
"implicit that §2 is the variable.\n"
"In Lean, we use the keyword §3 to describe a function by placing the name of the variable\n"
"after §4.\n"
"So §5 defines the function that takes input §6 and outputs §7 in Lean.\n"
"\n"
"Watch out! Some of these lemmas have names with a dot and starting with a capital letter\n"
"like §8 (these ones prove continuity of a combination of functions)\n"
"and some have an underscore and start with a lowercase letter like §9\n"
"(these ones state that some specific function is continuous)."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Start`
#: Game.Levels.Intro
msgid "Welcome to Lean! In this Introduction World you will learn some of the basics.\n"
"We strongly encourage you to carefully read the explanations in this panel.\n"
"Click §0 to begin."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∃`
#. §1: `use`
#. §2: ```
#. ⊢ ∃ n : ℕ, n + 1 = 1
#. ```
#. §3: `n`
#. §4: `use 0`
#. §5: `0 + 1 = 1`
#. §6: `zero_add`
#: Game.Levels.Intermediate.L03_Three
msgid "When the goal is to prove an existential §0, we can\n"
"supply the witness (an example that has the desired property)\n"
"using the tactic §1.\n"
"\n"
"For example :\n"
"If the goal is\n"
"§2\n"
"then we have to take §3 to be zero, so we type §4.\n"
"The remaining goal will then be that\n"
"§5\n"
"which is provable with §6."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rewrite [add_zero x] at hx`
#. §1: `rewrite`
#. §2: `add_zero`
#. §3: `x`
#. §4: `x`
#. §5: `rewrite [add_zero] at hx`
#: Game.Levels.Intro.L02_Two
msgid "Type §0 as a first step of the proof.\n"
"In fact, in this situation the §1 tactic can infer that the argument of §2 should be §3,\n"
"so one could leave out the argument §4, i.e. simply write §5."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∧`
#. §1: `constructor`
#: Game.Levels.Intermediate.L03_Three
msgid "When given a goal that is an §0 (and) of two propositions, the §1 tactic\n"
"will produce two goals, one for each side, that can be solved individually."
msgstr ""

